\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because any two distinct tiles lie either in different rows or in different columns.  \nEvery cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs either to \\(T_i^{\\text{h}}\\) (if \\(i<j\\)) or to \\(T_j^{\\text{v}}\\) (if \\(i>j\\)).  \nThus the complement of the diagonal is tiled by exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Structural decomposition\n\nFix any permutation \\(p\\) and any tiling attaining the minimum for the given \\(p\\).  \nLet the uncovered square in the first row be at column \\(c\\); write\n\\[\na=c-1,\\qquad b=n-c,\n\\]\nso \\(a+b=n-1\\).  \n\nDefine the following sets of rows:\n\\[\nL=\\{\\,i>1\\mid p(i)<c\\,\\},\\qquad R=\\{\\,i>1\\mid p(i)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\nBecause each column contains exactly one uncovered square, we have \\(|L|=a\\) and \\(|R|=b\\).\n\nConsider the seven regions obtained by intersecting the row‑sets \\(\\{1\\},L,R\\) with the column‑sets \\(\\{c\\},\\{1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\\{c+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nOnly the following six regions can contain cells (the region \\(\\{1\\}\\times\\{c\\}\\) is the uncovered square itself):\n\n| Region | Description | Size |\n|---|---|---|\n| \\(A\\) | Row 1, columns \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) | \\(1\\times a\\) |\n| \\(B\\) | Row 1, columns \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\) | \\(1\\times b\\) |\n| \\(C\\) | Rows \\(L\\), columns \\(c,\\dots ,n\\) | \\(a\\times(b+1)\\) |\n| \\(D\\) | Rows \\(R\\), columns \\(1,\\dots ,c\\) | \\(b\\times(c)\\) |\n| \\(E\\) | Rows \\(L\\), columns \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) | \\(a\\times a\\) |\n| \\(F\\) | Rows \\(R\\), columns \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\) | \\(b\\times b\\) |\n\nThe regions \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) each contain a permutation of uncovered squares (the restriction of \\(p\\) to the corresponding rows and columns).  \nThe other four regions \\(A,B,C,D\\) are **hole‑free** rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 1 – each non‑empty hole‑free region needs its own tile  \n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a tile \\(T\\) contains a cell of region \\(A\\) and also a cell of any other region among \\(\\{B,C,D\\}\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) lies in row 1 and columns \\(<c\\) while any other such region contains a column \\(\\ge c\\) or a row \\(\\ge2\\), the column interval of \\(T\\) must contain column \\(c\\) and the row interval must contain row 1. Consequently \\((1,c)\\in T\\), contradicting the rule that no tile may cover an uncovered square.  \nThe same argument applies to any pair of distinct regions among \\(\\{A,B,C,D\\}\\).  \nHence a tile cannot intersect two distinct hole‑free regions; each non‑empty region among \\(A,B,C,D\\) requires at least one tile. ∎  \n\nConsequently any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2 &\\text{if }a=0\\text{ or }b=0,\\\\\n4 &\\text{if }a>0\\text{ and }b>0,\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles for the hole‑free part.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 2 – the two permutation subboards need at least \\(f(a)\\) and \\(f(b)\\) tiles  \n\n*Proof.*  \nRegion \\(E\\) is an \\(a\\times a\\) board whose uncovered squares are exactly \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\in L\\}\\); this is a permutation of size \\(a\\).  \nBy definition of \\(f\\), covering its complement needs at least \\(f(a)\\) tiles.  \nThe same reasoning applies to region \\(F\\), giving at least \\(f(b)\\) tiles. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Recurrence for the minimum\n\nCombining Lemma 1 and Lemma 2, any tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board satisfies\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\;\\ge\\;\n\\min_{a+b=n-1}\n\\Bigl(\nf(a)+f(b)\n\\;+\\;2\\;+\\;2\\mathbf1_{a>0,b>0}\n\\Bigr), \\tag{3}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf1_{a>0,b>0}=1\\) if both \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Induction giving the lower bound \\(f(n)\\