would have j ≤ c2 < r1 ≤ i, implying j < i, contradicting i < j. So intervals cannot be disjoint, so they intersect, thus diagonal cell exists. So the claim is correct.\n\nThus any rectangle must be entirely above diagonal (rows < columns) or below diagonal (rows > columns). So the tiling splits into two independent triangular regions.\n\nUpper bound: They construct horizontal strips for each row i (i from 1 to n-1) covering cells (i, i+1..n). That's a rectangle of size 1 x (n-i). And vertical strips for each column j (j from 1 to n-1) covering cells (j+1..n, j). These are disjoint because each tile lies either in a distinct row (horizontal) or distinct column (vertical). Indeed, horizontal tile H_i covers row i only, columns > i. Vertical tile V_j covers column j only, rows > j. So they don't intersect. Also they cover all non-diagonal cells: For any cell (x,y) with x<y, it's in row x and column y, and since y > x, it's covered by H_x (since H_x includes columns > x). For x>y, it's covered by V_y (since V_y includes rows > y). So all non-diagonal cells covered. Number of tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. So upper bound is valid.\n\nLower bound: They consider the set S+ of cells (i,i+1) for i=1..n-1 (first super-diagonal). Claim: any rectangle above diagonal can contain at most one of those cells. Proof: Suppose rectangle contains (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i<j. Then row interval includes [i,j] and column interval includes [i+1,j+1]. Since i+1 ≤ j, intervals intersect, thus diagonal cell (t,t) inside rectangle, contradiction. So each rectangle can contain at most one S+ cell. Since there are n-1 cells in S+, need at least n-1 rectangles for upper triangular region. Similarly for lower triangular region using S- = (i+1,i). So total at least 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nThus lower bound matches upper bound, so minimal number is 2n-2. For n=2025, answer 4048.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow check Level 2: Reasoning validity. The solution uses reduction via permutation, geometric reas