w covers columns at positions 5 (original column 2), 3 (original column 3), and 4 (original column 4). In the new ordering, these are positions 2,3,4? Wait, after swapping column 2 with column 5, the new ordering is: column 1 stays at position 1, column 5 moves to position 2, column 3 stays at position 3, column 4 stays at position 4, column 2 moves to position 5. So the rectangle originally covering columns 2-4 (original indices) now covers columns at positions 2 (col5), 3 (col3), 4 (col4). So the rectangle now covers columns 2-4 in the new ordering (positions). Indeed, after swapping, the rectangle's columns become contiguous again because the set of positions of the original columns 2,3,4 after the permutation are positions {2,3,4} (since column 5 moved to position 2, column 3 to 3, column 4 to 4). But is this always the case? Let's examine: For any permutation sigma, the image of an interval [c1,c2] under sigma may not be an interval in the order of sigma's values. However, if we physically reorder columns according to sigma, we reorder the positions of columns. The rectangle's columns after reordering will be those columns that originally were in [c1,c2], now placed at positions sigma(c1), sigma(c1+1), ..., sigma(c2). But these positions may not be contiguous in the new ordering because sigma is not monotonic. However, the rectangle's shape in the new board is defined by the set of columns at positions sigma(c1),...,sigma(c2). The shape may not be a rectangle because there may be gaps between these positions. But the solution says \"its column interval is permuted\". This is ambiguous. However, the key point is that the number of tiles needed for any permutation of uncovered squares is the same as for diagonal case because we can permute columns (or rows) to map any configuration to diagonal. But does this preserve the property that each tile is a rectangle? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have a tiling T for a configuration U (set of uncovered squares). We