citly require that the tile's interior is a contiguous block of unit squares; but a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines is necessarily a contiguous block of unit squares. Because a rectangle's sides are straight lines, and the grid lines are the lines between rows and columns. If you have a set of rows that are not contiguous, the shape's sides would not be straight lines; they'd have steps. So the shape would not be a rectangle. So the transformation must preserve contiguity.\n\nThus the reduction is not valid. But perhaps the solution's reduction is not needed for the lower bound; they could have argued directly that any tiling must have at least n-1 tiles above diagonal and n-1 below diagonal, regardless of where uncovered squares are, because you can always reorder rows and columns to bring uncovered squares to diagonal while preserving the tiling shape? But we just argued that reordering columns may break shape. However, we could reorder rows and columns simultaneously to bring uncovered squares to diagonal while preserving shape? Let's think: Suppose we have uncovered squares at positions (i,p(i)). If we permute columns by sigma = p^{-1}, we map uncovered squares to diagonal. But we also need to permute rows by identity (i stays i). So we only permute columns. That may break shape. However, we could also permute rows by sigma (i.e., apply sigma to rows as well). Then uncovered squares become (sigma(i), sigma(p(i))) = (sigma(i), i). That's not diagonal. So not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, we could apply a row permutation pi = p to rows and column permutation sigma = p^{-1} to columns. Then uncovered squares become (p(i), p^{-1}(p(i))) = (p(i), i). That's not diagonal either.\n\nThus the reduction may not be possible while preserving rectangle property.\n\nBut maybe the solution's reduction is not necessary for the lower bound: The lower bound argument uses the first super-diagonal S+ = {(i,i+1)}. If the uncovered squares are not on diagonal, we could still co