                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Model the problem:** The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix; i.e., there is exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \n2. **Lower bound:** Show that any rectangular tile can completely cover the right‑hand side of at most one row (and similarly the left‑hand side of at most one column). Since there are \\(n-1\\) rows with a non‑empty right side and \\(n-1\\) columns with a non‑empty left side, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed on each side, giving a lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n3. **Construction attaining the bound:** Place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   - The squares to the right of the diagonal (the “upper” region) form a staircase shape. It can be tiled by the \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles  \n     \\[\n     R_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots ,n .\n     \\]  \n   - The squares to the left of the diagonal (the “lower” region) form the reflected staircase and can be tiled by the \\(n-1\\) horizontal rectangles  \n     \\[\n     C_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,n .\n     \\]  \n   These \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are disjoint and cover all squares except the diagonal holes.  \n4. **Conclusion:** The lower bound and the construction match, so the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with an \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by row–column pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain **exactly one** uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other squares are to be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles, no two tiles overlapping.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Decomposition into “right’’ and “left’’ regions\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) consists of the squares to the **right** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\);  \n\\(L_i\\) consists of the squares to the **left** of it.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nR^+=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i,\\qquad L^-=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(R^+\\) or to \\(L^-\\), and a tile can lie wholly in one of these two regions (otherwise it would contain an uncovered square).  \nHence the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=t_R+t_L,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t_R\\) (resp. \\(t_L\\)) is the number of tiles needed to partition \\(R^+\\) (resp. \\(L^-\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A lower bound for \\(t_R\\)\n\nConsider any tile \\(T\\subseteq R^+\\).  \nLet its set of rows be a contiguous interval \\(I_T\\) and its set of columns a contiguous interval \\(J_T\\).  \nBecause \\(T\\) contains no uncovered square, for every \\(i\\in I_T\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)<\\min J_T .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, the leftmost column of \\(T\\) is at least \\(\\max_{i\\in I_T}\\sigma(i)+1\\).\n\nIf \\(T\\) were to **cover the whole right side of two distinct rows** \\(i\\neq i'\\), then we would have  \n\n\\[\nJ_T=[\\sigma(i)+1,n]=[\\sigma(i')+1,n],\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(i')\\), impossible because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Each tile in }R^+\\text{ can completely cover the right side of at most one row.}}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows whose right side is non‑empty (the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) has none).  \nBy (2) at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all of \\(R^+\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nt_R\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the transposed board (swap rows and columns) gives\n\n\\[\nt_L\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAdding (3) and (4) yields the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 2n-2\\, } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   A construction attaining the bound\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal:\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,j-1\\) are contiguous, each \\(R_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  \nThe sets \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are disjoint and their union is exactly \\(R^+\\).  \nHence \\(t_R=n-1\\).\n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\); the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) partitions \\(L^-\\).  \nThus \\(t_L=n-1\\).\n\nTogether the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_j\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\}\\) cover every square except the diagonal holes, and no two rectangles overlap.  \n\nTherefore the bound (5) is tight:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Evaluation for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) into (7),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Conclusion\n\nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place is \\(4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
