d square, the uncovered squares must lie in distinct columns.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Partition of the covered cells  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) consists of the cells strictly to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\); \\(R_i\\) consists of the cells strictly to the right of that hole.  \nThe set of all covered cells is the disjoint union  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad \nR=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nThus every covered unit square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Upper bound – a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place at most two horizontal tiles:\n\n* If \\(p(i)>1\\) we place the tile  \n\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le p(i)-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is a \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) rectangle covering exactly the cells of \\(L_i\\).\n\n* If \\(p(i)<n\\) we place the tile  \n\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\(1\\times(n-p(i))\\) rectangle covering exactly the cells of \\(R_i\\).\n\nThese tiles are axis‑aligned, their interiors are pairwise disjoint (different rows or separated by the uncovered cell), and together they cover every cell of \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\) (no right tile). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{i\\mid p(i)>1\\}=n-1,\\qquad \n\\#\\{i\\mid p(i)<n\\}=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the construction uses precisely \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \nFor \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\n**Left side.**  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the unique row with \\(p(i_j)=j+1\\) (such a row exists because \\(p\\) is a permutation).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\).  \n\nAny rectangular tile that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) cannot contain column \\(j+1\\), otherwise it would also cover the uncovered cell \\((i_j,j+1)\\). Consequently the rightmost column of that tile is exactly \\(j\\).  \n\nSince a tile has a single rightmost column, distinct values of \\(j\\) require distinct tiles. Hence at least one tile is needed for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\); thus any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n**Right side.**  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(i'_j\\) be the unique row with \\(p(i'_j)=j-1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i'_j,j)\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \n\nA tile covering this cell cannot contain column \\(j-1\\), otherwise it would cover the uncovered cell \\((i'_j,j-1)\\). Hence the leftmost column of that tile is exactly \\(j\\).  \n\nAgain, distinct values of \\(j\\) force distinct tiles, so at least one tile is required for each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\); therefore any tiling of \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nBecause the sets \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, a tile that serves the left side cannot also serve the right side (its rightmost column is fixed, preventing it from covering any cell to the right of a hole). Consequently the total number of tiles in any tiling of the whole board is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Minimality  \n\nThe construction of §3 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of §4 shows that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example 