ctually the argument only ensures each j requires a distinct tile, but could a tile cover multiple left cells from different rows? Wait, tile is rectangular, axis-aligned, covering a set of unit squares. If a tile covers (i_j, j) and also covers (i_{j'}, j') for j' != j, then its rightmost column would be max(j, j'), but the tile cannot include column j+1 (or j'+1). But if j < j', then the tile's rightmost column is j', but then it would include column j+1? Actually if tile covers (i_j, j) and (i_{j'}, j'), we need to examine if it's possible. The tile's rightmost column must be at least max(j, j'). But the tile cannot contain column j+1 (for covering (i_j, j)). So the tile's rightmost column must be <= j. But if j' > j, then the tile's rightmost column must be >= j', contradiction. So tile cannot cover two left cells with different j values. So each j requires a distinct tile. This is implicitly understood but not explicitly spelled out. However, this is a justification gap? The solution states \"Since a tile has a single rightmost column, distinct values of j require distinct tiles.\" That is a concise justification; it's acceptable. So no critical errors.\n\nPotential minor issues: The solution uses \"Because p is a permutation, exactly one row has p(i)=1 (no left tile) and exactly one row has p(i)=n (no right tile).\" Actually there could be multiple rows with p(i)=1? No, p is a permutation of {1,...,n}, so each value appears exactly once. So exactly one row has p(i)=1, exactly one row has p(i)=n. So correct.\n\nThus Level 3: No critical errors, maybe some justification gaps but acceptable. So final verdict PASS.\n\nNow we need to output JSON with fields: verdict, confidence, issues (list), answer_correctness, reasoning.\n\nConfidence: high, maybe 0.99.\n\nIssues: we can list any justification gaps found. There might be none. But we could note minor justification gap: The lower bound argument could be more explicit about why a tile cannot cover two left cells with different j values, but it's still valid. According to guidelines, justification gaps are acceptable and do not cause FAIL. So we can list them as \"Justification Gap: lower bound argument could elaborate why distinct columns require distinct tiles.\" Or we could leave issues empty. The guidelines: \"issues\" is list of issues found. If none, can be empty array. But we might want to include any justification gaps. The guidelines say \"list any presentation issues found (Justification Gaps or Critical Errors in logic chain)\". So we should list any justification gaps. There's at least one minor gap: The argument that each column j requires a distinct tile is okay, but the reasoning that a tile cannot cover two left cells from different rows is not fully spelled out. That could be considered a justification gap. However, the solution says \"Since a tile has a single rightmost column, distinct values of j require distinct tiles.\" That is a concise justification. It may be considered sufficient. It's not a missing step that invalidates logic. So maybe no issues. But we can still list \"Justification Gap: The lower bound argument could be more explicit about why a tile cannot cover multiple left cells from different rows.\" That is a minor gap. According to guidelines, justification gaps are acceptable. So we can include it.\n\nThus JSON:\n\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PASS\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.99,\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"Justification Gap\",\n      \"description\": \"The lower bound argument could elaborate why a tile cannot cover two left cells from different rows, but the reasoning is essentially correct.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answer_correctness\": \"CORRECT\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"The solution correctly identifies the uncovered squares as a permutation matrix, provides a construction using 2n-2 tiles, and proves a matching lower bound using valid counting arguments. All methods are valid and the answer 4048 is correct.\"\n}\n\nMake sure JSON format is 