---------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the number of right\u2011tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define the **right\u2011corner cell**\n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\text{R}}=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R_i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, because precisely one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{c_i^{\\text{R}}\\}=n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Every right\u2011tile contains exactly one right\u2011corner cell, and distinct right\u2011tiles contain distinct right\u2011corner cells.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\subseteq R\\) be a right\u2011tile.  \nFor each row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we have \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\) (otherwise a column \\(\\le\\sigma(i)\\) would be present and the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would be covered).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\ni^{*}=\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}_{i\\in[a,b]}\\sigma(i) .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(c>\\sigma(i^{*})\\).  \nIf \\(c\\ge\\sigma(i^{*})+2\\), the cell \\((i^{*},\\sigma(i^{*})+1)\\) would be uncovered and could be added to \\(T\\) (the enlarged rectangle would still lie in \\(R\\)), contradicting the maximality of the column interval.  \nHence \\(c=\\sigma(i^{*})+1\\), and the cell \\((i^{*},\\sigma(i^{*})+1)=c_{i^{*}}^{\\text{R}}\\) belongs to \\(T\\).\n\nIf another right\u2011corner cell \\(c_{j}^{\\text{R}}\\) also lay in \\(T\\), then \\(\\sigma(j)+1\\ge c=\\sigma(i^{*})+1\\), so \\(\\sigma(j)\\ge\\sigma(i^{*})\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma(i^{*})\\) is maximal on \\([a,b]\\), we must have \\(\\sigma(j)=\\sigma(i^{*})\\), whence \\(j=i^{*}\\) (because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation).  \nThus \\(T\\) contains exactly one right\u2011corner cell. \u220e\n\nBecause the right\u2011tiles are disjoint, distinct tiles contain distinct right\u2011corner cells.  \nConsequently the number of right\u2011tiles is at least the number of right\u2011corner cells, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{right\u2011tiles}\\}\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------