                "content": "answer.\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Model the uncovered squares.**  \n   Let the uncovered squares be \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \n   The covered squares split into two disjoint regions:  \n   - the **upper region** \\(U=\\{(i,j):i<\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\}\\) (cells above the hole in their column), and  \n   - the **lower region** \\(L=\\{(i,j):i>\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\}\\) (cells below the hole in their column).\n\n2. **Corner argument for a lower bound.**  \n   In \\(U\\) each column \\(j\\) contains exactly \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)-1\\) cells; the multiset of column heights is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   Consequently \\(U\\) possesses exactly \\(n-1\\) *corner cells* – cells that have no neighbour of \\(U\\) directly below **and** no neighbour directly to the right.  \n   Any axis‑parallel rectangle contained in \\(U\\) contains at most one such corner (otherwise it would have to contain a cell outside \\(U\\)). Hence any partition of \\(U\\) into rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. The same reasoning applies to \\(L\\), giving another \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   Therefore **any tiling** that respects the condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) place a horizontal tile covering \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) – a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \n   - For each column \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) place a vertical tile covering \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\) – a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n\n   These \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, lie entirely in the upper or lower region, and together cover all squares except the diagonal ones.  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction match, so the minimum number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA tiling must satisfy that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(r_j\\) the row index of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\); i.e. \\(r_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\).  \nFor a cell \\((i,j)\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n* \\((i,j)\\in H\\) – the cell is uncovered;\n* \\(i<r_j\\) – the cell lies **above** the uncovered square of its column;\n* \\(i>r_j\\) – the cell lies **below** the uncovered square of its column.\n\nHence the set of covered squares splits into two disjoint regions\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<r_j\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>r_j\\},\n\\]\nthe *upper* and *lower* regions respectively.  \nA rectangle cannot contain a cell of \\(U\\) together with a cell of \\(L\\), because any column intersected by such a rectangle would contain both a row above and a row below the hole, forcing the rectangle to also contain the hole itself.  \nThus every tile (rectangle) lies entirely in \\(U\\) or entirely in \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the number of tiles in \\(U\\)\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) the number of cells of \\(U\\) in that column equals \\(r_j-1\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{r_j-1\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsequently the column heights of \\(U\\) are a permutation of \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\nA cell \\((i,j)\\in U\\) is called a **corner** of \\(U\\) if it has no neighbour of \\(U\\) directly below and no neighbour directly to its right; equivalently,\n\\[\n(i+1,j)\\notin U\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i,j+1)\\notin U .\n\\]\nBecause column \\(j\\) contains exactly \\(r_j-1\\) cells, the unique cell \\((r_j-1,j)\\) (when \\(r_j>1\\)) is a corner.  \nThus there are exactly \\(n-1\\) corners in \\(U\\) (the column with height \\(0\\) contributes none).\n\nNow let \\(R\\subseteq U\\) be any axis‑parallel rectangle.  \nLet \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max})\\) be the cell of \\(R\\) with maximal row index and maximal column index (the bottom‑rightmost cell of \\(R\\)).  \nIf \\((i_{\\max}+1,j_{\\max})\\in U\\) or \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max}+1)\\in U\\), then \\(R\\) could be enlarged while staying inside \\(U\\), contradicting the choice of \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max})\\).  \nHence \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max})\\) is a corner of \\(U\\).  \n\nConversely, a rectangle cannot contain two distinct corners.  \nIndeed, suppose \\(R\\) contained corners \\((i_1,j_1)\\) and \\((i_2,j_2)\\) with \\(i_1<i_2\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) is a product of intervals, it also contains the cell \\((i_2,j_1)\\).  \nBut column \\(j_1\\) has height exactly \\(i_1\\); therefore \\((i_2,j_1)\\notin U\\), a contradiction.  \nThus **each rectangle in a partition of \\(U\\) contains at most one corner**.\n\nBecause \\(U\\) has \\(n-1\\) corners, any partition of \\(U\\) into rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The same bound for the lower region \\(L\\)\n\nThe lower region \\(L\\) is defined symmetrically:\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>r_j\\}.\n\\]\nFor column \\(j\\) the number of cells of \\(L\\) equals \\(n-r_j\\); the multiset \\(\\{n-r_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is again \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nHence \\(L\\) also possesses exactly \\(n-1\\) corners (now the cells \\((r_j+1,j)\\) for those columns with \\(r_j<n\\)).  \nRepeating the argument of §1 shows that any rectangle lying in \\(L\\) contains at most one corner of \\(L\\); consequently any partition of \\(L\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nSince the covered squares are precisely \\(U\\cup L\\) and the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the board with rectangles that avoids the uncovered squares must contain at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. choose \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(U=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\) (the strict upper triangle) and \\(L=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\) (the strict lower triangle).\n\n*For the upper triangle.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.\n\n*For the lower triangle.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{C_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, lie entirely in \\(U\\) or \\(L\\), and together cover all squares except the diagonal ones.  Hence the lower bound is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nThe minimal possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\n* Verify that every statement in the “Detailed Solution” follows logically from the previous ones and that no step relies on an unproven claim.  \n* Ensure all mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters.  \n* Confirm that the lower‑bound argument correctly counts corners and that 