ghbour of \\(U\\) directly below and no neighbour directly to its right; equivalently,\n\\[\n(i+1,j)\\notin U\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i,j+1)\\notin U .\n\\]\nBecause column \\(j\\) contains exactly \\(r_j-1\\) cells, the unique cell \\((r_j-1,j)\\) (when \\(r_j>1\\)) is a corner.  \nThus there are exactly \\(n-1\\) corners in \\(U\\) (the column with height \\(0\\) contributes none).\n\nNow let \\(R\\subseteq U\\) be any axis‑parallel rectangle.  \nLet \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max})\\) be the cell of \\(R\\) with maximal row index and maximal column index (the bottom‑rightmost cell of \\(R\\)).  \nIf \\((i_{\\max}+1,j_{\\max})\\in U\\) or \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max}+1)\\in U\\), then \\(R\\) could be enlarged while staying inside \\(U\\), contradicting the choice of \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max})\\).  \nHence \\((i_{\\max},j_{\\max})\\) is a corner of \\(U\\).  \n\nConversely, a rectangle cannot contain two distinct corners.  \nIndeed, suppose \\(R\\) contained corners \\((i_1,j_1)\\) and \\((i_2,j_2)\\) with \\(i_1<i_2\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) is a product of intervals, it also contains the cell \\((i_2,j_1)\\).  \nBut column \\(j_1\\) has height exactly \\(i_1\\); therefore \\((i_2,j_1)\\notin U\\), a contradiction.  \nThus **each rectangle in a partition of \\(U\\) contains at most one corner**.\n\nBecause \\(U\\) has \\(n-1\\) corners, any partition of \\(U\\) into rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The same bound for the lower region \\(L\\)\n\nThe lower region \\(L\\) is defined symmetrically:\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>r_j\\}.\n\\]\nFor column \\(j\\) the number of cells of \\(L\\) equals \\(n-r_j\\); the multiset \\(\\{n-r_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is again \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nHence \\(L\\) also possesses exactly \\(n-1\\) corners (now the cells \\((r_j+1,j)\\) for those columns with \\(r_j<n\\)).  \nRepeating the argument of §1 shows that any rectangle lying in \\(L\\) contai