ht interval }R_i}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are (possibly empty) contiguous intervals of columns.\nA tile (axis‑parallel rectangle) cannot contain a hole, therefore a tile that\ncovers any cell of row \\(i\\) must be completely contained either in \\(L_i\\) or in\n\\(R_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the left side region\n\nDefine the *left side region*\n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\}=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i:=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i,c_i)\\) is the right‑most cell of \\(L_i\\); it belongs to \\(L\\) and\nlies in column \\(c_i\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(c_i\\;(p(i)>1)\\) are precisely the\nset \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); in particular they are all distinct.\n\nConsider any tiling of the whole board.  \nA tile that contains \\((i,c_i)\\) must have its column interval ending at\ncolumn \\(c_i\\); otherwise the tile would also contain the hole \\((i,p(i))\\).\nConsequently the rightmost column of a tile covering \\((i,c_i)\\) is uniquely\ndetermined by \\(c_i\\).\n\nSince the values \\(c_i\\) are distinct, the cells \\((i,c_i)\\) must belong to\npairwise different tiles.  Hence at least one tile is required for each\nnon‑empty left interval.  Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (the row whose hole\nlies in column 1), so there are \\(n-1\\) non‑empty left intervals.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling uses at least }n-1\\text{ tiles to cover }L.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound for the right side region\n\nDefine the *right side region*\n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) put  \n\n\\[\nd_i:=p(i)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i,d_i)\\) is the left‑most cell of \\(R_i\\) and lies in column \\(d_i\\).\nThe numbers \\(d_i\\;(p(i)<n)\\) are exactly \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\), again all\ndistinct.\n\nA tile containing \\((i,d_i)\\) must have its column interval beginning at\n\\(d_i\\); otherwise it would contain the hole \\((i,p(i))\\).  Hence the tiles\ncovering the cells \\((i,d_i)\\) are all distinct, and we need at least one\ntile for each non‑empty right interval.  Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\), so\nthere are \\(n-1\\) non‑empty right intervals.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling uses at least }n-1\\text{ tiles to cover }R.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nA tile cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) (otherwise it would contain a\nhole), so the total number of tiles in any tiling is at least the sum of the\ntwo lower bounds:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nFix any permutation \\(p\\).  For each row \\(i\\) do the following:\n\n* If \\(p(i)>1\\), place a tile covering the whole left interval \\(L_i\\); this\n  tile is the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) rectangle.\n\n* If \\(p(i)<n\\), place a tile covering the whole right interval \\(R_i\\); this\n  tile is  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{i\\}\\times\\{p(i)+1,p(i)+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\(1\\times(n-p(i))\\) rectangle.\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in different rows) and together\ncover every cell of the board except the \\(n\\) uncovered squares \\((i,p(i))\\).\nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) (no left tile) and one row has \\(p(i)=n\\) (no\nright tile); all other rows contribute two tiles.  Hence the total number of\ntiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound \\((4)\\) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Application to the given size\n\nFor 