he tile also must be contiguous in rows: it's a rectangle, so it covers a set of consecutive rows. So we could potentially have a tile that covers multiple rows, each with left intervals that all end at columns >= p(i)-1? Wait, the tile's rightmost column is p(i)-1, so for any row j that the tile covers, the tile's column interval is the same: [some left, p(i)-1]. For row j, the left interval L_j is columns 1..p(j)-1. If p(j)-1 >= p(i)-1 (i.e., p(j) >= p(i)), then the tile's column interval [left, p(i)-1] is a subset of L_j (since L_j includes columns up to p(j)-1). So the tile can be placed within L_j as well. So a tile could cover multiple rows i, i+1, ... as long as for each row j in that block, p(j) >= p(i). So the tile's rightmost column is the minimum of p(j)-1 across those rows. Actually the tile's rightmost column is determined by the row with smallest p(j)-1 among those rows. So the argument that each left interval requires its own tile might be flawed because we could combine left intervals from rows where p(i) is monotonic decreasing? Let's examine more concretely.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument: For each row i with p(i)>1, define c_i = p(i)-1. The cell (i,c_i) is the rightmost cell of L_i. They claim that a tile containing (i,c_i) must have rightmost column = c_i. Since a rectangle has a single rightmost column, distinct cells (i,c_i) and (i',c_i') with c_i != c_i' cannot belong to the same tile. This is not necessarily true: a tile could have rightmost column = max(c_i, c_i')? Actually a rectangle's rightmost column is a single column index. If a tile contains both (i,c_i) and (i',c_i'), then both cells must have column index <= tile's rightmost column. But the tile's rightmost column is the maximum column index among cells in the tile. So if c_i != c_i', the tile's rightmost column would be max(c_i, c_i'). But the tile also must not contain the uncovered square (i,p(i)) for row i, which is column c_i+1. If tile's rightmost column is > c_i, then