e uncovered square for row i. However, maybe p(i) is less than leftmost column of tile? But leftmost column of tile is at most column of left cell (which is <= p(i)-1). So leftmost column <= p(i)-1. Rightmost column >= column of right cell (>= p(j)+1). So the interval includes p(i) because p(i) is between p(i)-1 and p(j)+1 (if p(j)+1 > p(i)). But it's possible p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1? That would mean p(j) <= p(i)-2, so right cell column is <= p(i)-2, which is still left of p(i). But a right cell is defined as column >= p(j)+1, but if p(j) <= p(i)-2, then p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1, which is still left side of row i's uncovered square. But the right cell belongs to row j, not row i. The tile includes row j and row i. The column interval includes columns up to p(i)-1, which is left of p(i). So the tile does not include column p(i) for row i. However, does it include uncovered square for row j? Row j's uncovered column is p(j). Since tile's column interval includes columns up to p(i)-1, which might be > p(j) or < p(j). Let's analyze: p(j) <= p(i)-2, so p(j) <= p(i)-2 < p(i)-1. So p(j) is also left of p(i)-1, thus inside the column interval of tile. Since tile includes row j (because includes right cell at column >= p(j)+1, which is <= p(i)-1), the tile's column interval includes column p(j) for row j, which is uncovered. So tile would contain uncovered square for row j. So any tile that includes a left cell from one row and a right cell from another row will inevitably include the uncovered square for at least one of those rows. Therefore the argument that a tile cannot intersect both L and R is correct.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 seems valid.\n\nNow the construction: They claim to place a tile covering each left interval L_i (if non-empty) and each right interval R_i (if non-empty). That yields exactly 2n-2 tiles. But is that construction valid? They place each tile as a 1x(p(i)-1) rectangle for left side, and 1x(n-p(i)) rectangle for right side. These are horizontal strips of height 1 (i.e., covering a single row). Since each tile is in a distinct row, they are disjoint. They cover all squares except the uncovered squares. Indeed, each row i has at most two tiles: one covering left interval, one covering right interval. For rows where p(i)=1, left interval empty, no left tile; similarly for p(i)=n, no right tile. So total tiles = sum_{i} [1_{p(i)>1} + 1_{p(i)<n}] = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. So construction works.\n\nThus answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to verify the solution's reasoning: Are the methods valid? They use combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, argument about uniqueness of rightmost column, etc. All valid. They also provide a construction. So reasoning uses valid mathematical principles: combinatorial argument, proof by contradiction? Not exactly, but they use direct reasoning. That's valid.\n\nWe also need to check if there are any presentation issues that are critical errors. The solution appears fairly rigorous. There might be minor gaps: For the lower bound, they claim \"Since the tile must contain (i,c_i), its rightmost column is forced to be precisely c_i.\" This is justified as we argued. They also claim \"A rectangle has a single rightmost column; consequently two distinct cells (i,c_i) and (i',c_{i'}) with c_i != c_{i'} cannot belong to the same tile.\" That's correct. They also claim \"Hence each non‑empty left interval requires its own tile.\" That's correct.\n\nPotential minor justification gap: The argument that each non-empty left interval requires its own tile is based on the uniqueness of c_i. That is correct. However, they might need to argue that a tile covering left interval L_i must contain the cell (i,c_i). Actually they didn't explicitly argue that any tile covering L_i must contain the rightmost cell (i,c_i). But they used the fact that if a tile contains (i,c_i), then it cannot contain any other c_i'. But what if a tile covering L_i does not contain (i,c_i)? Could it be that L_i is covered by multiple