um number of tiles is \\(2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column; consequently the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.  \nDenote the set of uncovered squares by  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Normalisation\n\nThe board is invariant under independent permutations of rows and columns.  \nIf we permute the columns by \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\), the set \\(H\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince a permutation of columns merely relabels the columns, any tiling of the original board can be transformed into a tiling of the relabelled board with the same number of tiles, and vice‑versa.  \nHence we may (and shall) assume\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of the uncovered squares\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the hole \\((i,i)\\) splits the row into\n\n* a **left part** \\(L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\}\\) of length \\(i-1\\);\n* a **right part** \\(R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}\\) of length \\(n-i\\).\n\nAll squares that must be covered are precisely the union of the left parts and the right parts.\n\nA rectangle (tile) is a product \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of consecutive rows and columns, respectively.  \nIf a rectangle contains a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\) (a left‑part cell), then its column interval \\(J\\) must be a subset of \\([1,i-1]\\); otherwise the rectangle would also contain the hole \\((i,i)\\).  \nThus any rectangle covering left‑part cells is **left‑justified**: its column interval is of the form \\([1,c]\\) for some \\(c\\).  \