und is tight; therefore the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column; consequently the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.  \nDenote the set of uncovered squares by  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Normalisation\n\nThe board is invariant under independent permutations of rows and columns.  \nIf we permute the columns by \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\), the set \\(H\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince a permutation of columns merely relabels the columns, any tiling of the original board can be transformed into a tiling of the relabelled board with the same number of tiles, and vice\u2011versa.  \nHence we may (and shall) assume\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of the uncovered squares\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the hole \\((i,i)\\) splits the row into\n\n* a **left part** \\(L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\}\\) of length \\(i-1\\);\n* a **right part** \\(R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}\\) of length \\(n-i\\).\n\nAll squares that must be covered are precisely the union of the left parts and the right parts.\n\nA rectangle (tile) is a product \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of consecutive rows and columns, respectively.  \nIf a rectangle contains a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\) (a left\u2011part cell), then its column interval \\(J\\) must be a subset of \\([1,i-1]\\); otherwise the rectangle would also contain the hole \\((i,i)\\).  \nThus any rectangle covering left\u2011part cells is **left\u2011justified**: its column interval is of the form \\([1,c]\\) for some \\(c\\).  \nSimilarly, any rectangle covering right\u2011part cells is **right\u2011justified**, i.e. its column interval is \\([d,n]\\) for some \\(d\\).\n\nConsequently the covering problem separates into two independent sub\u2011problems:\n\n* cover all left\u2011part cells with left\u2011justified rectangles;\n* cover all right\u2011part cells with right\u2011justified rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound for the left side\n\nConsider the cell \\((k+1,k)\\) for a fixed \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nIn row \\(k+1\\) the hole is at column \\(k+1\\); therefore any rectangle that contains \\((k+1,k)\\) cannot contain column \\(k+1\\).  \nHence its column interval must be a subset of \\([1,k]\\), and because it contains column \\(k\\) its **rightmost** column is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus each \\(k\\) forces a distinct left\u2011justified rectangle whose rightmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nSince the rightmost column of a rectangle is unique, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At least } n-1 \\text{ left\u2011justified rectangles are necessary.}} \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the right side\n\nSymmetrically, for each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consider the cell \\((k-1,k)\\).  \nRow \\(k-1\\) has its hole at column \\(k-1\\); any rectangle covering \\((k-1,k)\\) must avoid column \\(k-1\\) and therefore its column interval must be a subset of \\([k,n]\\).  \nSince it contains column \\(k\\), its **leftmost** column is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nHence each \\(k\\) forces a distinct right\u2011justified rectangle whose leftmost column equals \\(k\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At least } n-1 \\text{ right\u2011justified rectangles are necessary.}} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Total lower bound\n\nFrom (2) and (3) any tiling of the board must use at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  For our concrete value \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2=4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are required at a minimum.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Construction attaining the bound\n\nDefine, for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\nR_k=\\{(i,n-k+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(L_k\\) is a vertical rectangle of width\u202f1 whose rows are exactly those rows whose left part contains column \\(k\\);*  \n*each \\(R_k\\) is a vertical rectangle of width\u202f1 whose rows are exactly those rows whose right part contains column \\(n-k+1\\).*\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_k\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because a column appears either in the left family (columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) or in the right family (columns \\(2,\\dots ,n\\)), never both, and the row intervals are chosen so that the diagonal row is omitted in each case.\n\nNow take any non\u2011diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n* If \\(j<i\\) (the cell lies left of the diagonal), then \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(i\\ge j+1\\); consequently \\((i,j)\\in L_j\\).\n* If \\(j>i\\) (the cell lies right of the diagonal), write \\(j=n-k+1\\) with \\(k=n-j+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Then \\(i\\le j-1=n-k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\).\n\nThus every non\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one of the rectangles defined above, while the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) belong to none of them.  Hence the set \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is a valid tiling consisting of exactly\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is achievable, so it is optimal.  For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
