lumn \\(c\\).  \n   For each \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   R_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   covering the cells above the diagonal in column \\(c\\).  \n\n   The \\(2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every non‑diagonal cell exactly once, and the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered. Thus the lower bound is achievable.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The minimum number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with the \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered square and each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Normalisation  \n\nPermuting the columns of the board by the permutation \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) sends the set \\(H\\) to  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA column permutation merely relabels the columns; any tiling of the original board can be transformed by the same column permutation into a tiling of the relabelled board with the same number of tiles, and vice‑versa.  \nHence we may **assume** that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Geometry of a tile that avoids the diagonal  \n\nA tile is a rectangle \\(T=I\\times J\\) where  \n\n\\[\nI=\\{r_1,r_1+1,\\dots ,r_2\\},\\qquad \nJ=\\{c_1,c_1+1,\\dots ,c_2\\}\n\\]\n\nare intervals of consecutive rows and columns.  \nIf \\(T\\) contains no cell of \\(D\\), then \\(I\\cap J=\\varnothing\\); otherwise a point \\((k,k)\\) with \\(k\\in I\\cap J\\) would be uncovered inside \\(T\\), contradicting (1).\n\nSince \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of integers, the condition \\(I\\cap J=\\varnothing\\) forces one of the two possibilities:\n\n* **Case L (left side).** \\(\\displaystyle \\min I > \\max J\\).  \n  Every row of \\(T\\) is larger than every column of \\(T\\); thus all cells of \\(T\\) lie **left** of the diagonal.\n\n* **Case R (right side).** \\(\\displaystyle \\max I < \\min J\\).  \n  Every row of \\(T\\) is smaller than every column of \\(T\\); thus all cells of \\(T\\) lie **right** of the diagonal.\n\nConsequently each tile belongs exclusively to one side; a tile cannot contain cells on both sides of the diagonal. \\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the left side  \n\nConsider the column indices \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nThe cell \\((c+1,c)\\) lies left of the diagonal, hence must be covered by a tile of type **L**.  \nLet this tile be \\(T=I\\times J\\).  \nBecause \\((c+1,c)\\in T\\), we have \\(c\\in J\\).  \nSince the diagonal cell \\((c+1,c+1)\\) is uncovered, column \\(c+1\\) cannot belong to \\(J\\); otherwise \\((c+1,c+1)\\in T\\).  \nTherefore the **rightmost** column of \\(J\\) is exactly \\(c\\).\n\nThus each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is the rightmost column of some left‑side tile.  \nDistinct tiles have distinct rightmost columns, so at least \\(n-1\\) left‑side tiles are required:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{left‑side tiles}\\}\\ge n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the right side  \n\nSymmetrically, for each column \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) the cell \\((c-1,c)\\) lies right of the diagonal and must be covered by a tile of type **R**.  \nLet this tile be \\(T=I\\times J\\).  \nBecause \\((c-1,c)\\in T\\), we have \\(c\\in J\\).  \nThe uncovered diagonal cell \\((c-1,c-1)\\) forces column \\(c-1\\notin J\\); otherwise \\((c-1,c-1)\\in T\\).  \nHence the **leftmost** column of \\(J\\) is exactly \\(c\\).\n\nConsequently each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the leftmost column of some right‑side tile, and distinct tiles have distinct leftmost columns. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{right‑side tiles}\\}\\ge n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Total lower bound  \n\nAdding (2) and (3) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{tiles}\\}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives the numeric bound \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine, for each \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nL_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe vertical rectangle of width 1 occupying column \\(c\\) and all rows **below** the diagonal.  \nDefine, for each \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nR_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe vertical rectangle of width 1 occupying column \\(c\\) and all rows **above** the diagonal.\n\n*Each \\(L_c\\) and each \\(R_c\\) is a legitimate tile (a rectangle of unit width).  \n*The families \\(\\{L_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_c\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because a column appears either in a left‑side tile (rows \\(>c\\)) or in a right‑side tile (rows \\(<c\\)), never both.  \n\nNow take any non‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n*If \\(j<i\\) then \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(i\\ge j+1\\); thus \\((i,j)\\in L_j\\).\n*If \\(j>i\\) then \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(i\\le j-1\\); thus \\((i,j)\\in R_j\\).\n\nHence every cell not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one of the tiles defined above, while the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) belong to none of them.  \nThe total number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n| \\{L_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1} | + | \\{R_c\\}_{c=2}^{n} | = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus the construction achieves the lower bound (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe minimum possible number of tiles equals the lower bound \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}.\n\\]"
