ain \\(i=a\\).  \nConsequently \\(d\\ge i-1=a-1\\); together with (2) this forces \\(d=a-1\\).  \n\nThus, if a rectangle in \\(L\\) contains a corner square, its top row is exactly that square’s row and its rightmost column is exactly that square’s column. In particular, **a rectangle can contain at most one corner square**.\n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) corner squares of \\(C_L\\) are all distinct and must be covered, any partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\ge n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2. The upper triangle needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nDefine the corner squares of the upper triangle  \n\n\\[\nC_U=\\{(i,i+1): i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset U .\n\\]\n\nEach \\((i,i+1)\\) is the **bottommost** square of column \\(i+1\\) and the **leftmost** square of row \\(i\\).  \n\nIf a rectangle \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\subset U\\), then because no square may lie on or below the diagonal we have  \n\n\\[\nc\\ge a+1. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRepeating the reasoning of §3.1 with (4) shows that a rectangle in \\(U\\) can contain at most one square of \\(C_U\\). Hence covering all \\(n-1\\) squares of \\(C_U\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }U\\ge n-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3. Combining the two halves  \n\nThe lower and upper triangles are disjoint, so the total number of rectangles in any tiling of the whole board minus the diagonal satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Optimality  \n\nFrom (1) we have a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles; from (6) we have a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the o