ontradicting the fact that it already\ncovers a cell of column 1 whose height is \\(n-1\\).  Hence a rectangle that covers\ncolumn 1 can contain **no other** column.  The same argument applied to column\n\\(2\\) (height \\(n-2\\)) shows that any rectangle covering column 2 cannot also\ncover any column of larger index, because those columns are shorter.  Continuing\ninductively, we see that each column \\(c\\) of the left region must be covered by\na distinct rectangle.\n\nThus at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to tile the left region \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The right region\n\nThe right region \\(R\\) is the transpose of \\(L\\); the same reasoning applied to\n\\(R\\) yields the identical lower bound: at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed\nto tile \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Global lower bound\n\nSince the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board must contain\nat least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nrectangles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this gives the bound \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nFix the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) that determines the holes.\nFor each column \\(j\\) let the hole be at row \\(r_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\).\n\n* **Upper tile in column \\(j\\).**  \n  If \\(r_j>1\\), place a vertical tile of size\n  \\[\n  1\\times(r_j-1)\n  \\]\n  covering the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<r_j\\}\\) (the cells of column \\(j\\)\n  that lie **above** the hole).\n\n* **Lower tile in column \\(j\\).**  \n  If \\(r_j<n\\), place a vertical tile of size\n  \\[\n  1\\times(n-r_j)\n  \\]\n  covering the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j<i\\le n\\}\\) (the cells of column \\(j\\)\n  that lie **below** the hole).\n\nEach tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle, the tiles are pairwise disjoint,\nand together they cover every c