on must be \\(B\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) splits the row into a left part\nand a right part:\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nDefine the *left region* and the *right region* by\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\nClearly \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\) and \\(L\\cup R=B\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Shape of the left region\n\nLet \\(r_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\); thus the hole in column \\(j\\) lies in row \\(r_j\\).\nConsider the rows ordered as \\(r_1,r_2,\\dots ,r_n\\).\nFor a fixed \\(j\\) we have\n\\[\nL_{r_j}=\\{(r_j,k)\\mid 1\\le k<j\\},\n\\]\nso the \\(j\\)-th row in this order contains exactly \\(j-1\\) cells of \\(L\\).\nConsequently the left region is the Ferrers diagram of the partition\n\\[\n( n-1,\\,n-2,\\,\\dots ,\\,2,\\,1,\\,0).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nIn particular, column \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)) of \\(L\\) has height\n\\[\nh_c=n-c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe heights \\(h_1,h_2,\\dots ,h_{n-1}\\) are all distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for tiling a staircase diagram\n\nLet a rectangle \\(T(I,J)\\) be placed inside the left region \\(L\\).\nBecause \\(J\\) is an interval of columns, the rectangle contains a whole set of\nconsecutive columns.  Its height equals \\(|I|\\) and its width equals \\(|J|\\).\n\nTake the leftmost column of \\(L\\), namely column \\(1\\).  By (2) its height is\n\\(h_1=n-1\\).  Any rectangle that contains a cell of column 1 cannot be taller\nthan \\(h_1\\); therefore its height is at most \\(n-1\\).  If a rectangle were to\ncontain **another** column, say column \\(c>1\\), then its height would have to be\nat most \\(\\min\\{h_1,h_c\\}=h_c<n-1\\), contr