 minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of rows by \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns also by \\([n]\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\([n]\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy renaming the columns (i.e. applying the column permutation \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\)) we may assume \\(\\sigma\\) is the identity.  \nHenceforth the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(C\\) be the set of covered squares:\n\\[\nC=\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus H.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Rectangles stay on one side of the diagonal)\n\nLet \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be an axis\u2011aligned rectangle (with \\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)) that contains no diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).  \nIf there existed \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(c\\le i\\le d\\), then the cell \\((i,i)\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nTherefore either \\(i<c\\) for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) or \\(i>d\\) for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\).  \nIn the first case every cell of \\(T\\) satisfies column\u202f\\(> \\)\u202frow, so \\(T\\subseteq R\\); in the second case every cell satisfies column\u202f\\(<\\)\u202frow, so \\(T\\subseteq L\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently any tiling of \\(C\\) splits into a tiling of the two disjoint regions  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(L\\))\n\nIn region \\(L\\) the \\(i\\)-th row contains exactly \\(i-1\\) squares; thus the set of positive row lengths of \\(L\\) is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), each length occurring once.\n\nFor a fixed \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_k=k+1\\).  \nRow \\(i_k\\) has length \\(k\\) and its rightmost cell in \\(L\\) is \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nConsider any rectangle that covers \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nBecause row \\(i_k\\) has no cell in column \\(k+1\\), the rectangle cannot contain column \\(k+1\\); hence its rightmost column is at most \\(k\\).  \nSince the rectangle must contain column \\(k\\) to cover \\((i_k,k)\\), its rightmost column is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus each \\(k\\) forces a rectangle whose rightmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nDifferent values of \\(k\\) give different rightmost columns, and a rectangle possesses a unique rightmost column.  \nHence at least one rectangle is required for each \\(k\\), i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to tile \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\))\n\nThe argument for \\(R\\) is symmetric.  \nIn \\(R\\) the \\(i\\)-th row contains \\(n-i\\) squares; the set of positive row lengths is again \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i'_k=n-k\\).  \nRow \\(i'_k\\) has leftmost cell in \\(R\\) at \\((i'_k,k+1)\\).  \nAny rectangle covering this cell cannot extend to column \\(k\\) (that column does not belong to the row), so its leftmost column is exactly \\(k+1\\).  \n\nConsequently each \\(k\\) forces a distinct rectangle (distinguished by its leftmost column), and at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(R\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the left region \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles, and by Lemma\u202f3 the right region \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \nSince the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole set \\(C\\) uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*For the left region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \n\\(V_j\\) contains exactly the cells of column \\(j\\) that belong to \\(L\\).\n\n*For the right region.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times(n-i)\\).  \n\\(H_i\\) contains exactly the cells of row \\(i\\) that belong to \\(R\\).\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different rows or different columns) and together cover all squares of \\(C\\).  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number of tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
