 rectangles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n\n   Place the uncovered squares on the diagonal.  \n\n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     covering all cells of \\(L\\) in column \\(j\\).\n\n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     covering all cells of \\(R\\) in row \\(i\\).\n\n   The \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different rows or different columns) and together cover every off‑diagonal cell. Thus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimal number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Denote the set of rows and columns by \\([n]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares\n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, and each column must also contain exactly one.  \nHence the uncovered squares are a set  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\([n]\\).  \n\nA permutation of the columns of the board does not change the number of tiles needed: applying the same column permutation to every tile yields a valid tiling of the permuted board with the same number of tiles.  Consequently we may assume \\(\\sigma\\) is the identity permutation.  Hence\n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nare the uncovered squares (the main diagonal).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nC=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus H\n\\]\n\nbe the set of covered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Rectangles cannot cross the diagonal\n\nConsider an axis‑aligned rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\qquad (1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n)\n\\]\n\nthat contains no diagonal cell.  \nIf there existed an integer \\(i\\) with \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i\\le d\\), then the cell \\((i,i)\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nTherefore either \\(b<c\\) (all rows of \\(T\\) are strictly above all its columns) or \\(a>d\\) (all rows are strictly below all its columns).  \n\nHence any rectangle avoiding the diagonal lies entirely in one of the two disjoint regions  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling of \\(C\\) consists of a tiling of \\(L\\) together with a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lower bound for the region \\(L\\)\n\nIn \\(L\\) the \\(i\\)-th row (\\(i\\ge2\\)) contains exactly \\(i-1\\) cells, namely the cells \\((i,1),\\dots,(i,i-1)\\).  \nIts rightmost cell is \\((i,i-1)\\).\n\n**Claim.** Any rectangle covering \\((i,i-1)\\) has rightmost column equal to \\(i-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle \\(T\\) covers \\((i,i-1)\\).  Its column interval is \\([c,d]\\) with \\(c\\le i-1\\le d\\).  If \\(d\\ge i\\), then column \\(i\\) belongs to \\(T\\); since row \\(i\\) is also in \\(T\\), the cell \\((i,i)\\) (a diagonal cell) would be covered, which is impossible.  Hence \\(d=i-1\\).  ∎\n\nConsequently, for each \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the cell \\((k+1,k)\\) (the rightmost cell of row \\(k+1\\)) must be covered by a rectangle whose rightmost column is exactly \\(k\\).  Different values of \\(k\\) give different rightmost columns, and a rectangle possesses a unique rightmost column.  Therefore at least one rectangle is required for each \\(k\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling of }L\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n------------