-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint: a vertical tile lies in a single column, a horizontal tile lies in a single row, and a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) occupies rows \\(>j\\) while a horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) occupies columns \\(>i\\); thus no cell belongs to both a vertical and a horizontal tile.  \n\nTogether they cover every off‑diagonal cell, because:\n\n* If \\((i,j)\\in L\\) then \\(i>j\\) and \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).\n* If \\((i,j)\\in R\\) then \\(i<j\\) and \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).\n\nThe diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered, satisfying the requirement that each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered square.\n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{V_j\\}|+|\\{H_i\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number of tiles.  Hence the minimal possible number of rectangular tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
