 \n   \u2013 Non\u2011hole: in the first family the column is \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) while the hole in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(i+1)\\); similarly for the second family.  \n   \u2013 Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) (with sides on grid lines) contains two squares of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).  \n     Without loss of generality let the two squares be \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) and \\((k,\\sigma(k+1))\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \n     Then the row interval of \\(R\\) contains all rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,k\\); in particular it contains row \\(i+1\\).  \n     The column interval of \\(R\\) contains column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) because the square \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \n     Consequently the cell \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) \u2013 which is precisely the hole in row \\(i+1\\) \u2013 lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids all holes.  \n     An analogous argument works if the two squares belong to different families. \u220e  \n\n   Because each rectangle can contain **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\), any covering of all non\u2011hole squares needs at least \\(|\\mathcal{C}| = 2(n-1)\\) rectangles.  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the lemma shows that fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles are impossible.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the minimum number of tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The holes form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each of the \\(n\\) rows must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each of the \\(n\\) columns must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell  \n\n\\[\n(i,\\sigma(i))\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row that contains the uncovered square in that column.\n\n*If \\(r_j>1\\)* place a **vertical** tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(r_j<n\\)* place a **vertical** tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese tiles are axis\u2011aligned rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines.  \nIn a fixed column the two tiles (when both exist) are separated by the hole \\((r_j,j)\\); therefore they are disjoint.  \nTiles belonging to different columns occupy different columns, so they are also disjoint.  \nConsequently the whole board is tiled by these rectangles, and every uncovered square is exactly the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nHow many tiles are used?  \nA column whose hole lies in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)) contributes only the lower tile; a column whose hole lies in the bottom row (\\(r_j=n\\)) contributes only the upper tile.  \nAll other \\(n-2\\) columns contribute two tiles.  \nHence the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-2)\\cdot 2+1 = 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nDefine the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}= \\bigl\\{(i,\\sigma(i+1))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n            \\;\\cup\\;\n            \\bigl\\{(i+1,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).*  \n\n*   **Cardinality.** The two families in (1) each contain \\(n-1\\) squares, and they are disjoint because their row indices differ; therefore \\(|\\mathcal{C}| = 2(n-1)\\).\n\n*   **No element of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) is a hole.**  \n    In the first family the column is \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) while the hole in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(i+1)\\); similarly for the second family.\n\n*   **Two squares of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) cannot lie in the same hole\u2011free rectangle.**  \n\n    *Proof.*  \n    Assume a rectangle \\(R\\) (sides on grid lines) contains two distinct squares of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).  \n    By symmetry we may suppose they are  \n\n    \\[\n    A=(i,\\sigma(i+1)),\\qquad B=(k,\\sigma(k+1))\n    \\]\n\n    with \\(i<k\\).  \n    The row interval of \\(R\\) contains all rows from \\(i\\) up to \\(k\\); in particular it contains row \\(i+1\\).  \n    The column interval of \\(R\\) contains column \\(\\sigma(i+1)\\) because it contains the point \\(A\\).  \n    Hence the cell \\((i+1,\\sigma(i+1))\\) \u2013 which is exactly the uncovered square (hole) in row \\(i+1\\) \u2013 lies inside \\(R\\).  \n    This contradicts the assumption that \\(R\\) avoids all holes.  \n\n    The same argument works if the two squares belong to different families, because any two squares of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) share either a row or a column that forces the inclusion of a hole. \u220e  \n\nBecause a rectangle that avoids all holes can contain **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\), any covering of the whole board by such rectangles must use at least \\(|\\mathcal{C}| = 2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\nThus **no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of Section\u202f3 shows that fewer tiles are impossible.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
