eed that for each row k in the interval, σ(k) lies outside the column interval. That is, for each k in [i, j+1], σ(k) ∉ [min(σ(i+1), σ(j)), max(σ(i+1), σ(j))].\n\nThus the column interval must be disjoint from the set {σ(k) : k in interval}. But the column interval is defined by the endpoints σ(i+1) and σ(j). So we need that for all k in interval, σ(k) is either less than both endpoints or greater than both endpoints. That would mean that the set of σ-values for rows in the interval is either all less than min(σ(i+1), σ(j)) or all greater than max(σ(i+1), σ(j)). But since σ(i+1) and σ(j) themselves are in that set (since i+1 and j are in the interval), we have at least two values inside the interval. So the set cannot be completely outside. Actually the column interval is defined by the minimum and maximum of σ(i+1) and σ(j). So those two values are at the endpoints of the interval. So the interval includes them. So the set of σ-values for rows i+1 and j are inside the interval. So the rectangle will contain holes at (i+1,σ(i+1)) and (j,σ(j)). Wait careful: The hole at row i+1 is at column σ(i+1). That column is one of the endpoints of the interval, so the hole (i+1,σ(i+1)) is in the rectangle (since rectangle includes row i+1 and column σ(i+1)). Similarly, the hole at row j is at column σ(j), which is also an endpoint. So rectangle includes those holes. So any rectangle containing A_i and B_j (i<j) must contain holes at (i+1,σ(i+1)) and (j,σ(j)). However, we need to verify that the rectangle includes those rows and columns. The rectangle includes rows from i to j+1. So row i+1 is included. The column interval includes column σ(i+1) (since it's one of the endpoints). So (i+1,σ(i+1)) is inside rectangle. Similarly, row j is included (since j ≤ j+1). Column σ(j) is included. So indeed rectangle contains holes.\n\nThus the argument holds for any i<j: rectangle includes holes at (i+1,σ(i+1)) and (j,σ(j)). For i>j, similar reasoning.\n\nThus any rectangle containing a sq