                    "reasoning_content": "We need to read the problem and solution, then apply hierarchical decision tree.\n\nProblem: 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned) covering unit squares, each tile's sides on grid lines, each unit square covered at most once. Determine minimum number of tiles needed so that each row and each column has exactly one unit square not covered by any tile.\n\nSolution: They define n=2025. They argue holes form a permutation matrix. Then they construct a tiling with 2n-2 tiles: For each column j, find row r_j where hole is. Place vertical tile covering cells above hole (if r_j>1) and vertical tile covering cells below hole (if r_j<n). So each column gets either 1 or 2 tiles, total 2n-2. Then they prove lower bound: define set C of 2(n-1) squares, each rectangle can contain at most one of them, so need at least 2(n-1) tiles. So minimal number is 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nWe need to check answer correctness: answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think: The problem asks for minimum number of tiles. The solution claims 2n-2 = 4048. Is that indeed minimal? Let's verify reasoning.\n\nWe need to verify if the lower bound argument is valid: They define C = { (i, σ(i+1)) for i=1..n-1 } ∪ { (i+1, σ(i)) for i=1..n-1 }. So they take squares adjacent to holes in row and column sense. They claim these squares are not holes. That's true because holes are at (i, σ(i)). So (i, σ(i+1)) has column σ(i+1) which is different from σ(i) because σ is a permutation, so column differs. Similarly (i+1, σ(i)). So they are not holes.\n\nThen they claim that any rectangle that avoids all holes can contain at most one element of C. They prove that if rectangle contains two squares from C, then it must contain a hole. They consider two squares from same family: (i, σ(i+1)) and (k, σ(k+1)). Suppose i<k. Then rectangle covering both must contain row i+1 and column σ(i+1). Then (i+1, σ(i+1)) is a hole (since hole in row i+1 is at column σ(i+1