\n|S|=2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll cells of \\(S\\) are covered (they are not uncovered squares).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma – a rectangle contains at most one cell of \\(S\\)  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\quad\\text{Let }R\\text{ be an axis‑aligned rectangle that does not contain any square of }U.\n\\text{ Then }|R\\cap S|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).  \nWrite these cells as \\((i,p(i)+\\varepsilon_i)\\) and \\((j,p(j)+\\varepsilon_j)\\) where \\(\\varepsilon_i,\\varepsilon_j\\in\\{-1,+1\\}\\) indicate “left’’ (\\(-1\\)) or “right’’ (\\(+1\\)).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(i\\le j\\).\n\nLet the row interval of \\(R\\) be \\([a,b]\\) and the column interval be \\([c,d]\\); thus\n\\[\na\\le i\\le j\\le b,\\qquad c\\le p(i)+\\varepsilon_i,\\;p(j)+\\varepsilon_j\\le d .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe consider three cases.\n\n*Case 1: \\(\\varepsilon_i=\\varepsilon_j=+1\\) (both right neighbours).*  \nThen \\(p(i)<p(i)+1\\le p(j)+1\\). Hence \\(c\\le p(i)+1\\) and \\(d\\ge p(j)+1\\).  \nSince \\(p(i)<p(j)\\) (the two columns are distinct), the integer \\(p(j)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nc\\le p(i)+1\\le p(j)\\le p(j)+1\\le d .\n\\]\n\nThus the column \\(p(j)\\) lies in \\([c,d]\\). Because row \\(j\\) lies in \\([a,b]\\), the square \\((j,p(j))\\in U\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids all uncovered squares.\n\n*Case 2: \\(\\varepsilon_i=\\varepsilon_j=-1\\) (both left neighbours).*  \nNow \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\) belong to \\([c,d]\\) with \\(p(i)\\neq p(j)\\).  \nAssume \\(p(i)<p(j)\\); then \\(p(i)-1<p(j)-1\\) and the interval \\([c,d]\\) contains the integer \\(p(i)\\) (since \\(p(i)= (p(i)-1)+1\\) and \\(p(i)\\le p(j)-1\\)).  \nConsequently \\((i,p(i))\\in U\\) lies in \\(R\\), again a contradiction.\n\n*Case 3: \\(\\varepsilon_i\\neq\\varepsilon_j\\) (one left, one right neighbour).*  \nSuppose \\(\\varepsilon_i=-1\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_j=+1\\) (the opposite ordering is symmetric).  \nThen \\([c,d]\\) contains the two numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)+1\\).  \nBecause \\(p(i)\\le p(j)\\) or \\(p(i)>p(j)\\), in either situation the interval \\([c,d]\\) must contain at least one of the columns \\(p(i)\\) or \\(p(j)\\).  \nIf it contains \\(p(i)\\), the square \\((i,p(i))\\in U\\) lies in \\(R\\); if it contains \\(p(j)\\), the square \\((j,p(j))\\in U\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \nBoth possibilities contradict the assumption that \\(R\\) avoids \\(U\\).\n\nAll three cases lead to a contradiction, therefore a rectangle avoiding all uncovered squares can contain at most one cell of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile is a rectangle that avoids the uncovered squares, hence by the Lemma each tile covers at most one element of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=2n-2\\) by (3), any tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any feasible tiling uses }\\ge 2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThus the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- **Disjointness.**  \n  Each \\(H_i\\) occupies only row \\(i\\) and columns strictly larger than \\(i\\); each \\(V_j\\) occupies only column \\(j\\) and rows strictly larger than \\(j\\).  \n  Hence a horizontal tile and a vertical tile can intersect only at a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i=j\\), but such squares are precisely the uncovered diagonal squares and are therefore not part of any tile.  \n  Two horizontal tiles lie in distinct rows, and two vertical tiles lie in distinct columns, so they never overlap.  \n\n- **C