l rows of \\(T\\), one of the two alternatives must hold for **every** \\(i\\in R(T)\\). Hence each tile is of one of the two types:\n\n* **left tile:** \\(c_2<\\min_{i\\in R(T)}\\sigma(i)\\);\n* **right tile:** \\(c_1>\\max_{i\\in R(T)}\\sigma(i)\\).\n\nConsequently the set of squares left of the holes,\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\nmust be tiled by left tiles, and the set of squares right of the holes,\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\]\nmust be tiled by right tiles.  The two families of tiles are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   A lower bound for the number of left (and right) tiles\n\nConsider the left region \\(L\\).  Row \\(i\\) of \\(L\\) contains exactly \\(a_i\\) squares; thus the multiset \\(\\{a_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) because the numbers \\(\\sigma(i)\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nExactly one row has \\(a_i=0\\) (the row where \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\)); the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows have the distinct positive lengths \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\quad\\text{Let }B\\text{ be a Ferrers board whose positive row lengths are } \n\\lambda_1>\\lambda_2>\\dots >\\lambda_k.\n\\text{ Any tiling of }B\\text{ by axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least }k\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  \nFor each distinct length \\(\\lambda_t\\) let \\(r_t\\) be the first row whose length equals \\(\\lambda_t\\).  \nThe cell \\((r_t,\\lambda_t)\\) (the rightmost cell of that row) is a *corner* of the board.  \nIf a rectangle \\(Q\\) contained two distinct corners \\((r_i,\\lambda_i)\\) and \\((r_j,\\lambda_j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) (so \\(\\lambda_i>\\lambda_j\\)), then the column interval of \\(Q\\) would contain both \\(\\lambda_j\\) and \\(\\lambda_i\\), and the row interval would contain rows \\(r_i,\\dots ,r_j\\).  \nConsequently \\(Q\\) would also contain the cell \\((r_j,\\lambda_i)\\).  \nSince \\(\\lambda_i>\\lam