\(\\lambda_i>\\lambda_j\\), column \\(\\lambda_i\\) lies **outside** row \\(r_j\\)\u2019s length, so \\((r_j,\\lambda_i)\\) is not a square of the Ferrers board.  \nThus a rectangle cannot contain two distinct corners.  \nBecause there are \\(k\\) corners, at least \\(k\\) rectangles are required. \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma to \\(L\\): the positive row lengths are the distinct integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); hence \\(k=n-1\\).  \nTherefore any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) left tiles.\n\nThe right region \\(R\\) has row lengths \\(b_i=n-1-a_i\\).  The multiset \\(\\{b_i\\}\\) is again \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with exactly one zero (the row where \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\)).  Hence the same argument shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) right tiles.\n\nConsequently **any** tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   A tiling attaining the lower bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) tile.  The row \\(i=1\\) has no left tile because its left segment is empty.\n\n*Right tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  The row \\(i=n\\) has no right tile.\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint, each lies entirely on the appropriate side of the diagonal hole, and together they cover every square of the board except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares.  \nThus we have used  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles, exactly the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Conclusion\n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction show that the smallest possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
