h column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nDefine  \n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus the covered squares are precisely \\(L\\cup R\\), and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (rectangles cannot cross the diagonal)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and which contains no uncovered square.  \nWrite the row interval of \\(T\\) as \\(I=[a,b]\\) and its column interval as \\(J=[c,d]\\) (both inclusive).  \nFor any row \\(i\\in I\\) the uncovered square in that row is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le p(i)\\le d\\) then \\((i,p(i))\\in T\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence for each \\(i\\in I\\) either \\(p(i)<c\\) (the hole lies left of \\(T\\)) or \\(p(i)>d\\) (the hole lies right of \\(T\\)).  \n\nSuppose there exist rows \\(i_1,i_2\\in I\\) with \\(p(i_1)<c\\) and \\(p(i_2)>d\\).  \nSince the values \\(\\{p(i):i\\in I\\}\\) are distinct integers, there must be some \\(i_3\\in I\\) with\n\\(c\\le p(i_3)\\le d\\), contradicting the previous paragraph.  \nTherefore **all** rows of \\(I\\) satisfy the same inequality; either \\(p(i)<c\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\) or \\(p(i)>d\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\).  \n\nConsequently \\(T\\) lies completely to the left of every hole in its rows (i.e. \\(T\\subseteq L\\)) or completely to the right (i.e. \\(T\\subseteq R\\)). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause of Lemma\u202f1 the tiling problem splits into two independent subproblems: partition \\(L\\) into rectangles, and partition \\(R\\) into rectangles.  \nWe treat \\(L\\); the same arguments apply to \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Structure of \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the number of squares of \\(L\\) in that row equals \\(p(i)-1\\).  \nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{p(i)-1:1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThus the rows of \\(L\\) have **pairwise distinct** lengths, and there is precisely one row of each length \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (lower bound for \\(L\\))\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the unique row satisfying \\(p(i_j)=j+1\\) (the hole in that row lies immediately to the right of column \\(j\\)).  \nThe cell \\(a_j:=(i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\).  \n\nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) that contains \\(a_j\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) extended to column \\(j+1\\), it would contain the hole \\((i_j,j+1)\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence the rightmost column of \\(R\\) is at most \\(j\\); because \\(a_j\\) lies in column \\(j\\), the rightmost column is exactly \\(j\\).  \n\nThus each rectangle covering a cell of \\(L\\) can be assigned a **rightmost column**, and the cell \\(a_j\\) forces a rectangle whose rightmost column is precisely \\(j\\).  \nSince the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) are distinct, we need at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles to cover all cells of \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (optimal construction for \\(L\\))\n\nReorder the rows so that the hole columns increase: i.e. arrange the rows in the order\n\\[\np(i_1)<p(i_2)<\\dots <p(i_n).\n\\]\nIn this order the \\(k\\)-th row (for \\(k\\ge2\\)) contains exactly the cells \\((i_k,1),(i_k,2),\\dots ,(i_k,k-1)\\), a contiguous horizontal segment.  \nTake each such segment as a separate rectangle; the first row (length\u202f0) needs no rectangle.  \nWe obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles that partition \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy sy