tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   For each column \\(j\\) with \\(r_j>1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   R_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\},\n   \\]  \n\n   and for each column with \\(r_j<n\\) place  \n\n   \\[\n   R_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n   \\]  \n\n   These rectangles are disjoint (they lie in different columns or are separated by the\n   unique hole in their column) and together cover every unit square except the\n   \\(n\\) holes.  Their total number is exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum\n   possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the board by\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); a unit square is denoted by the ordered pair \\((i,j)\\).\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square,\nthe set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a\npermutation  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nConsequently the set of **covered** squares is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tiling is a\npartition of \\(\\mathcal C\\) into such rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Critical cells\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j:=p^{-1}(j)\\qquad(1\\le j\\le n)\n\\]\n\nbe the row containing the hole in column \\(j\\).  \nIf \\(r_j>1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nu_j:=(r_j-1,j)\n\\]\n\nlies immediately **above** the hole; if \\(r_j<n\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nd_j:=(r_j+1,j)\n\\]\n\nlies immediately **below** the hole.  \nWe call the cells \\(u_j\\) (when they exist) *upper critical cells* and the\ncells \\(d_j\\) (when they exist) *lower critical cells*.  \nBecause the map \\(j\\mapsto r_j\\) is a permutation, the set\n\\(\\{r_j-1\\mid r_j>1\\}\\) consists of the \\(n-1\\) integers \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\);\nsimilarly \\(\\{r_j+1\\mid r_j<n\\}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Hence there are exactly\n\\(n-1\\) upper critical cells and \\(n-1\\) lower critical cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 – shape of a rectangle containing a critical cell  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangle (with sides on grid lines) that contains no hole.\nIf \\(T\\) contains an upper critical cell \\(u_j=(r_j-1,j)\\) then the bottommost\nrow of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(r_j-1\\).  Similarly, if \\(T\\) contains a lower\ncritical cell \\(d_j=(r_j+1,j)\\) then the topmost row of \\(T\\) is exactly\n\\(r_j+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains \\(u_j\\) and also a row \\(i\\ge r_j\\).  Since column \\(j\\)\nbelongs to \\(T\\), the cell \\((r_j,j)\\) would also belong to \\(T\\); but\n\\((r_j,j)\\) is a hole, contradicting the hypothesis.  Hence no row of \\(T\\)\nlies below \\(r_j-1\\), i.e. the bottommost row of \\(T\\) equals \\(r_j-1\\).  The\nstatement for a lower critical cell is symmetric. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 2 – different columns force different rectangles  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nIf \\(j\\neq k\\) and both columns have an upper critical cell, then the rectangles\nrequired by Lemma 1 for \\(u_j\\) and for \\(u_k\\) are distinct.  The same holds\nfor lower critical cells.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, to the contrary, that a single rectangle \\(T\\) contains both\n\\(u_j=(r_j-1,j)\\) and \\(u_k=(r_k-1,k)\\).  Without loss of generality\n\\(r_j<r_k\\).  By Lemma 1 the bottommost row of \\(T\\) is \\(r_k-1\\); therefore\n\\(T\\) contains every row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le r_k-1\\), in particular the row\n\\(i=r_j\\).  Since column \\(j\\) belongs to \\(T\\), the cell \\((r_j,j)\\) would be\nin \\(T\\); but \\((r_j,j)\\) is a hole, a contradiction.  Hence the two rectangles\nmust be different.  The argument for lower critical cells is identical. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nExactly one column has \\(r_j=1\\) (the column whose hole lies in the top row);\nthis column possesses **no** upper critical cell.  All other \\(n-1\\) columns have\none, so there are \\(n-1\\) upper critical cells.  By Lemma 2 each of them forces a\ndifferent rectangle, whence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary.\n\nSimilarly, exactly one column has \\(r_j=n\\) (the hole in the bottom row), so\nthere are \\(n-1\\) lower critical cells, each demanding a distinct rectangle.\nThus at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed.\n\nConsequently any tiling of the board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define the two vertical strips  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU_j &:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }r_j>1,\\\\[2mm]\nD_j &:=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }r_j<n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(U_j\\) (resp. \\(D_j\\)) is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.\nBecause a hole occupies the unique square \\((r_j,j)\\), the sets \\(U_j\\) and\n\\(D_j\\) are disjoint, and for distinct columns the strips lie in different\ncolumns, so all these rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\nEvery covered square \\((i,j)\\in\\mathcal C\\) satisfies either \\(i<r_j\\) or\n\\(i>r_j\\); in the first case it belongs to \\(U_j\\), in the second to \\(D_j\\).\nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{U_j\\mid r_j>1\\}\\cup\\{D_j\\mid r_j<n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers **all** squares of \\(\\mathcal C\\) exactly once.  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{j\\mid r_j>1\\}+\\#\\{j\\mid r_j<n\\}=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality and final computation  \n\nThe lower bound of Section 4 and the explicit tiling of Section 5 coincide;\ntherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the required minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
