square in column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition of the covered squares\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i<r_j\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i>r_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sets \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the holes), \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) form a partition of the whole \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A rectangle cannot meet both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle (rows \\(a\\le\\cdots\\le b\\), columns \\(c\\le\\cdots\\le d\\)) that contains no hole.  \nFix a column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le r_j\\le b\\) then \\((r_j,j)\\) would be a hole inside \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence for every such column either \\(b<r_j\\) (the whole rectangle lies **above** the hole in column \\(j\\)) or \\(a>r_j\\) (the whole rectangle lies **below** the hole in column \\(j\\)).  \n\nIf the rectangle contained a point of \\(A\\) (i.e. a cell with row \\(<r_j\\)) then for that column we must have \\(b<r_j\\).  \nConsequently the same inequality holds for **all** columns of the rectangle, so the rectangle is entirely contained in \\(A\\).  \nAnalogously, if the rectangle contains a point of \\(B\\) it is entirely contained in \\(B\\).  \nThus a hole‑free rectangle is wholly in \\(A\\) or wholly in \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The shapes of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) the condition \\(i<r_j\\) holds exactly for those columns whose uncovered square lies in a row larger than \\(i\\).  \nSince there are precisely \\(n-i\\) rows larger than \\(i\\), there are exactly \\(n-i\\) such columns.  \nHence row \\(i\\) of \\(A\\) contains exactly \\(n-i\\) cells.  \nConsequently the row lengths of \\(A\\) are  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\;n-2,\\;\\dots ,\\;1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) is a Ferrers board (a Young diagram) of the staircase shape (1).  \n\nA symmetric argument shows that row \\(i\\) of \\(B\\) contains exactly \\(i-1\\) cells, i.e. the row lengths of \\(B\\) are  \n\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for a staircase Ferrers board\n\nConsider a Ferrers board whose row lengths are (1).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) let \\((i,c_i)\\) be the **right‑most** cell of that row.  \nBecause the row lengths strictly decrease, the columns \\(c_i\\) are distinct and satisfy  \n\n\\[\nc_1>c_2>\\dots >c_{n-1}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma.** *Any rectangle contained in the board can contain at most one of the cells \\((i,c_i)\\).*  \n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains two distinct right‑most cells \\((i,c_i)\\) and \\((i',c_{i'})\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \nSince \\(c_i>c_{i'}\\), the column interval of \\(R\\) contains the column \\(c_i\\); the row interval of \\(R\\) contains the row \\(i'\\).  \nTherefore \\(R\\) also contains the cell \\((i',c_i)\\).  \nBut row \\(i'\\) ends at column \\(c_{i'}<c_i\\); consequently \\((i',c_i)\\) is **not** a cell of the board, contradicting the assumption that \\(R\\) lies inside the board. ∎  \n\nThe set \\(\\{(i,c_i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\) has cardinality \\(n-1\\).  \nBy the lemma each rectangle covers at most one of them, so any tiling of the board needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nApplying the same argument to the board \\(B\\) (use the left‑most cell of each row, which also form a set of size \\(n-1\\) with the analogous property) shows that any tiling of \\(B\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound\n\nBecause a rectangle cannot intersect both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) (Section 2), a tiling of the whole board uses a collection of rectangles for \\(A\\) and a collection for \\(B\\).  \nConsequently the total number of rectangles is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Upper part.* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles cover exactly the cells of \\(A\\) (the cells above the diagonal).\n\n*Lower part.* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles cover exactly the cells of \\(B\\) (the cells below the diagonal).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together they cover every unit square except the diagonal cells, and each row and each column contain precisely one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \nThus we have a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (4) and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
