istinct cells of \\(C\\), say \\((i,c_i)\\) and \\((i',c_{i'})\\) with \\(i<i'\\), then the rectangle would also contain \\((i',c_i)\\), which is not a cell of the board (row \\(i'\\) stops at column \\(c_{i'}<c_i\\)).  \n   Hence **each rectangle can cover at most one cell of \\(C\\)**, and because \\(|C|=n-1\\) we need at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles to tile the board.  \n   The same argument applied to the left‑most cells of each row of \\(B\\) shows that any tiling of \\(B\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n6. **Global lower bound.**  \n   Any tiling of the whole \\(n\\times n\\) board therefore uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]  \n   rectangles.\n\n7. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((1,j),\\dots ,(j-1,j)\\) (the cells above the diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((j+1,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\) (the cells below the diagonal).  \n\n   The first family gives \\(n-1\\) tiles, the second family gives another \\(n-1\\) tiles; altogether \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   They are pairwise disjoint, cover every cell except the diagonal, and each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell).  \n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is achievable, so it is optimal. For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor a column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition of the covered squares\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i<r_j\\},\\qquad   \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i>r_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sets \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the holes), \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) form a partition of the whole \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A rectangle cannot meet both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle (rows \\(a\\le\\cdots\\le b\\), columns \\(c\\le\\cdots\\le d\\)) that contains no hole.  \nFix a column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le r_j\\le b\\) then \\((r_j,j)\\) would be a hole inside \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence for every such column either \\(b<r_j\\) (the whole rectangle lies **above** the hole in column \\(j\\)) or \\(a>r_j\\) (the whole rectangle lies **below** the hole in column \\(j\\)).  \n\nIf the rectangle contained a point of \\(A\\) (i.e. a cell with row \\(<r_j\\)) then for that column we must have \\(b<r_j\\).  \nConsequently the same inequality holds for **all** columns of the rectangle, so the rectangle is entirely contained in \\(A\\).  \nAnalogously, if the rectangle contains a point of \\(B\\) it is entirely contained in \\(B\\).  \nThus a hole‑free rectangle is wholly in \\(A\\) or wholly in \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The shapes of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) the condition \\(i<r_j\\) holds exactly for those columns whose uncovered square lies in a row larger than \\(i\\).  \nSince there are precisely \\(n-i\\) rows larger than \\(i\\), there are exactly \\(n-i\\) such columns.  \nHence row \\(i\\) of \\(A\\) contains exactly \\(n-i\\) cells.  \nConsequently the row lengths of \\(A\\) are  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\;n-2,\\