                "content": ".\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  Let \\(n=2025\\).  For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(c_i\\) the column of the unique uncovered square in that row.  The set \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) because each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square.  \n\n2. **Lower bound.**  In row \\(i\\) the uncovered square at column \\(c_i\\) splits the row into a left segment \\([1,c_i-1]\\) and a right segment \\([c_i+1,n]\\).  A rectangular tile cannot contain the uncovered square, hence any tile that lies in row \\(i\\) must be wholly contained in one of these two segments.  Consequently each non‑empty segment needs at least one tile.  All rows except the one with \\(c_i=1\\) have a non‑empty left segment, and all rows except the one with \\(c_i=n\\) have a non‑empty right segment.  Therefore any tiling uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.  \n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  Choose any permutation for the uncovered squares; the identity permutation \\((c_i=i)\\) is convenient.  For each row \\(i\\) place a horizontal tile covering the left segment \\([1,i-1]\\) (if it is non‑empty) and a horizontal tile covering the right segment \\([i+1,n]\\) (if it is non‑empty).  These tiles are axis‑parallel rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint (different rows), and together they cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.  Exactly two rows (the first and the last) receive only one tile, all other rows receive two, giving precisely \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n\n4. **Verification of the column condition.**  Because the uncovered squares form a permutation, each column \\(j\\) contains exactly the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\); all other squares of column \\(j\\) lie either in a left‑segment tile (if the row index exceeds \\(j\\)) or in a right‑segment tile (if the row index is smaller than \\(j\\)).  Hence each column also has exactly one uncovered unit square.  \n\nSince the lower bound and the construction match, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause every column must also contain exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \\pi(i)=c_i\n\\]\n\nis a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nFix a row \\(i\\).  The uncovered square \\((i,c_i)\\) separates the row into two\ncontiguous intervals of squares:\n\n* the **left segment**  \n  \\[\n  L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<c_i\\},\n  \\]\n* the **right segment**  \n  \\[\n  R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid c_i<j\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\nBoth \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) may be empty; they are empty precisely when\n\\(c_i=1\\) or \\(c_i=n\\), respectively.\n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; consequently a tile\nlying in row \\(i\\) must consist of a set of consecutive columns.\nIf a tile intersected both \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\), then, because the set of\ncolumns of the tile is an interval, it would also contain column \\(c_i\\),\nthe uncovered square, which is forbidden.  Hence **any tile that occupies\nrow \\(i\\) is contained entirely in \\(L_i\\) or entirely in \\(R_i\\).**  \n\nTherefore each non‑empty segment \\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) requires at least\none distinct tile.  Exactly one row has \\(c_i=1\\) (the row whose uncovered\nsquare lies in column 1), so there are \\(n-1\\) rows with a non‑empty left\nsegment.  Likewise exactly one row has \\(c_i=n\\), so there are \\(n-1\\) rows\nwith a non‑empty right segment.  Consequently any admissible tiling uses at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi\\) to be the identity, i.e. \\(c_i=i\\) for all\n\\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\); each\ncolumn also contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) construct the following tiles:\n\n* if \\(i>1\\) (so that \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\)), place a horizontal tile  \n\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole left segment;\n* if \\(i<n\\) (so that \\(R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\)), place a horizontal tile  \n\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle covering the whole right segment.\n\nAll these tiles are axis‑parallel rectangles, they lie on distinct rows,\nhence they are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every unit square\nexcept the \\(n\\) uncovered ones \\((i,i)\\).  The rows \\(i=1\\) and\n\\(i=n\\) receive only one tile each (the right‑most and left‑most rows,\nrespectively); every other row receives two tiles.  Hence the total number\nof tiles is  \n\n\\[\n1+1+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification of the column condition  \n\nBecause the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\), column \\(j\\) contains exactly\nthe uncovered square \\((j,j)\\).  All other squares of column \\(j\\) lie either\nin a left‑segment tile (if the row index exceeds \\(j\\)) or in a\nright‑segment tile (if the row index is smaller than \\(j\\)).  Thus each\ncolumn also has precisely one uncovered unit square, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nEquation (1) shows that no tiling can use 