rows with p(i) < c must be before r_c. Similarly, for i_c to be before r_c, there must be at least one row before r_c with p(i) > c. But there could be rows after r_c with p(i) > c as well; that doesn't affect i_c. So scenario: all rows with p(i) < c are before r_c; some rows with p(i) > c are before r_c (i_c before r_c). This is possible: e.g., consider permutation where columns less than c appear in early rows, and column c appears later, and columns greater than c appear both early and later. But note that there are c-1 values less than c. If all those appear before r_c, then r_c must be at least c (since there are c-1 rows before r_c). That's possible. For i_c to be before r_c, there must be at least one value >c before r_c. Since there are n-c values >c, they could be placed anywhere. So it's possible that both i_c and i'_c are before r_c. But wait, i'_c is defined as max{i | p(i) < c}. If all rows with p(i) < c are before r_c, then i'_c is the largest of those rows, which is before r_c. So i'_c < r_c. i_c is the smallest row with p(i) > c. If there is a row before r_c with p(i) > c, then i_c < r_c. So i_c < r_c as well. So both i_c and i'_c are before r_c. But is that allowed? Let's test with a small example: n=5, c=3. Suppose p = [4,5,3,1,2] (i.e., row1 col4, row2 col5, row3 col3, row4 col1, row5 col2). Then r_3 = 3 (row 3). Values less than 3 are 1 and 2, appear at rows 4 and 5 (both after r_3). So i'_3 = max{i | p(i) < 3} = max{4,5} = 5 > r_3. So i'_c > r_c. So not both before. Let's try to construct a permutation where all values less than c appear before r_c. For c=3, we need values 1 and 2 before row where p(i)=3. So we need p(1)=1, p(2)=2, p(3)=3 maybe. Then p = [1,2,3,4,5] (identity). Then r_3 = 3. Values less than 3 appear at rows 1 and 2 (both before). So i'_3 = max{i | p(i) < 3} = 2 < r_3. Values greater than 3 are 4 and 5, appear at rows 4 and 5 (both after). So i_c = min{i | p(i) > 3} = 4 > r_3. So i_c > r_c. So they are on opposite sides. So in identity, i_c > r_c, i'_c < r_c.\n\nNow try to find a permutation where both i_c and i'_c are before r_c. That would require that there is at least one value >c before r_c, and all values <c also before r_c. But if all values <c appear before r_c, then there are c-1 rows before r_c. So r_c must be at least c. That's okay. For i_c to be before r_c, there must be at least one value >c among those first r_c-1 rows. Since there are n-c values >c, we can place some of them early. So it's possible. Let's try n=5, c=3 again. We need values less than 3 (1,2) before r_3, and also at least one value >3 (4 or 5) before r_3. So we need at least 3 rows before r_3 (since we need to place 1,2, and maybe 4). So r_3 must be at least 4. Let's try p = [1,2,4,3,5]. Then r_3 = 4 (row 4). Values less than 3: 1 at row1, 2 at row2, both before r_3. So i'_3 = max{i | p(i) < 3} = 2 < r_3. Values >3: 4 at row3, 5 at row5. The smallest row with p(i) >3 is row3 (i_c = 3 < r_3). So i_c = 3 < r_3. So both i_c and i'_c are before r_3. Indeed i_c = 3, i'_c = 2. So they are both before r_3. So we have a case where i_c and i'_c are both before r_c. In this case, the critical L cell is at (i_c, c) = (3,3). The critical R cell is at (i'_c, c) = (2,3). Both are above the uncovered square at (4,3). So they are in the same column, rows 2 and 3. Could a rectangle contain both? Yes, a rectangle covering column 3 and rows 2-3 (i.e., rows 2 to 3) would contain both critical cells. However, does that rectangle also contain the uncovered square at (4,3)? No, because the rectangle's row interval is [2,3] which does not include row 4. So it's allowed. So a tile could contain both critical cells of L and R for column c if both critical cells are on the same side of the uncovered square. So a tile could cover both a critical L cell and a critical R cell. The solution's lower bound argument counts each critical cell separately, assuming each tile can cover at most one critical cell in total (i.e., cannot cover both). But they only proved each tile can contain at most one critical cell of L, and at most one critical cell of R. They did not prove that a tile cannot contain both a critical L cell and a critical R cell. So the lower bound of (n-1)+(n-1) may be too high; the true lower bound might be n-1 + something else but maybe can be less.\n\nBut does the construction achieve the lower bound exactly? The construction uses identity permutation p(i)=i. In that case, for each column c, the uncovered square is at (c,c). Then L region consists of cells left of diagonal (i>j). R region consists of cells right of diagonal (i<j). The critical L cells: for each column c (1 <= c <= n-1), i_c = min{i | p(i) > c} = min{i | i > c} = c+1. So critical L cells are (c+1, c). The critical R cells: for each column c (2 <= c <= n), i'_c = max{i | p(i) < c} = max{i | i < c} = c-1. So critical R cells are (c-1, c). So for each column c, the critical L cell is at row c+1, column c; critical R cell is at row c-1, column c. For c >= 2, the critical R cell is at row c-1, column c; for c <= n-1, the critical L cell is at row c+1, column c. So for each column c (except maybe extremes), the critical L cell is below the diagonal (row > column), and the critical R cell is above the diagonal (row < column). They are on opposite sides of the diagonal (uncovered square at (c,c)). So they cannot be in the same rectangle without covering the diagonal cell, because any rectangle covering column c and rows from c-1 to c+1 would include row c (the uncovered square). So in the identity permutation case, a tile cannot contain both a critical L cell and a critical R cell for the same column. However, a tile could contain a critical L cell from column c and a critical R cell from a different column d. For example, a tile could include column c and column d, and rows that include the critical L cell of column c (row c+1) and the critical R cell of column d (row d-1). Could that be possible without covering uncovered squares? Possibly. Let's examine.\n\nIn the identity case, the critical L cell for column c is at (c+1, c). The critical R cell for column d is at (d-1, d). Suppose we have a rectangle covering columns from c to d (c < d). The rectangle's column interval includes columns c and d. The row interval must include rows c+1 and d-1. If c+1 <= d-1, the row interval [c+1, d-1] includes rows between them. Does it include any uncovered squares? The uncovered squares are at (i,i) for i=1..n. The rectangle includes rows from c+1 to d-1 and columns from c to d. So any uncovered square (i,i) with i in [c+1, d-1] and i in [c, d] (i.e., i in [c+1, d-1]) will be inside the rectangle (since both row and column i are in the intervals). So the rectangle would contain uncovered squares for all i in that range, which is not allowed. So the rectangle cannot have row interval covering a range that includes any row i where column i is also in the column interval. So to avoid uncovered squares, the rectangle must avoid any row i such that its column i is also in the column interval. That is, the rectangle's row interval and column interval must be such that they do not intersect on the diagonal. In other words, the rectangle must be entirely above the diagonal (i < j for all cells) or entirely below the diagonal (i > j). Because if it includes any cell above the diagonal and any cell below the diagonal, then there will be a diagonal cell in between. Actually, more precisely, if the rectangle includes both a cell with i < j and a cell with i > j, then there must be some i=j cell inside the rectangle (by continuity of intervals). Because the rectangle is contiguous in rows and columns. If the rectangle includes a cell (i1, j1) with i1 < j1, and a cell (i2, j2) with i2 > j2, then the row interval includes rows from i1 to i2 (or i2 to i1) and column interval includes columns from j1 to j2 (or j2 to j1). Since i1 < j1 and i2 > j2, we have i1 <= i2 and j2 <= j1? Let's think: Suppose i1 < j1 and i2 > j2