l of R, thus covering both a critical cell from each region, reducing the total needed. The solution's argument that total tiles >= (n-1)+(n-1) assumes that tiles covering L cannot also cover R. But as we argued, a tile could contain both a critical L cell and a critical R cell if those cells are in the same rectangle. But are there any constraints that prevent a tile from containing both a critical L cell and a critical R cell? Let's examine the definitions of critical cells: For L, critical cells are (i_c, c) where i_c = min{i | p(i) > c}. For R, critical cells are (i'_c, c) where i'_c = max{i | p(i) < c}. So each column c has a critical L cell at row i_c (topmost cell left of uncovered squares) and a critical R cell at row i'_c (bottommost cell right of uncovered squares). For column c, i_c is the smallest row where the uncovered column is to the right of column c; i'_c is the largest row where uncovered column is to the left of column c. Since p is a permutation, for each column c, there is exactly one row where p(i) = c (the uncovered square in column c). Let's denote that row as r_c (i.e., p(r_c) = c). Then for rows i < r_c, p(i) != c; they could be >c or <c. For rows i > r_c, p(i) != c. The critical L cell (i_c, c) is the smallest row where p(i) > c. The critical R cell (i'_c, c) is the largest row where p(i) < c. Are these rows distinct? Possibly they could be the same row if p(i) > c and p(i) < c? No, a row cannot have p(i) both >c and <c. So i_c and i'_c are distinct unless there is no row with p(i) > c or no row with p(i) < c. But there is at least one row with p(i) > c (since p is a permutation of 1..n, there are values >c). Similarly, there is at least one row with p(i) < c. So both i_c and i'_c exist. Could i_c > i'_c? Possibly. Let's think: i_c is the smallest row where p(i) > c. i'_c is the largest row where p(i) < c. Since p(i) > c and p(i) < c are mutually exclusive, i_c could be less than, equal to, or greater than i'_c? Let's examine. For a given