\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); a tile cannot intersect both regions because any rectangle containing a square of \\(L\\) and a square of \\(R\\) would also contain the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) of the same row, which is forbidden.  \nThus the set of tiles is the disjoint union of the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\).  \nIt suffices to show that each of \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Structure of the left region  \n\nThe numbers \\(\\{p(i)-1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nReorder the rows so that the values \\(p(i)-1\\) appear in non‑decreasing order.  \nAfter this reordering the set \\(L\\) becomes\n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(L'\\) is the Young diagram (staircase) consisting of the cells strictly below the main diagonal of an \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \nThe shape \\(L'\\) contains exactly the \\(n-1\\) cells\n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich lie on the “staircase” (the cells immediately left of the diagonal).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Lemma: a rectangle in \\(L'\\) contains at most one cell of \\(S\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R=[r_1,r_2]\\times[c_1,c_2]\\) (with integer indices) is contained in \\(L'\\) and contains two distinct cells \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((i',i'-1)\\) of \\(S\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \nThen \\(c_1\\le i-1\\le c_2\\) and \\(c_1\\le i'-1\\le c_2\\); also \\(r_1\\le i\\le r_2\\) and \\(r_1\\le i'\\le r_2\\).  \nConsequently the cell \\((i,i')\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(i'\\)) belongs to \\(R\\) because \\(i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) and \\(i'\\in[c_1,c_2]\\).  \nBut \\(i'<i'\\) implies \\(i'\\ge i+1\\), so \\(i'\\ge i+1>i\\); hence the column index exceeds the row index, i.e. \\(i'\\