red pairs \\(\\{X\\times Y,\\;Y\\times X\\}\\).  \nEach pair covers all unordered pairs \\(\\{i,j\\}\\) with \\(i\\in X,\\;j\\in Y\\); in graph‑theoretic language it is a **complete bipartite subgraph** of the undirected complete graph \\(K_n\\) with parts \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\).\n\nConsequently, the unordered pairs \\(\\{\\{i,j\\}\\mid i\\neq j\\}\\) (the edge set of \\(K_n\\)) are partitioned into some number \\(m\\) of complete bipartite subgraphs.  \nThe total number of tiles equals \\(2m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound – Graham–Pollak theorem\n\nWe prove that any such partition of the edges of \\(K_n\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) bipartite subgraphs; therefore any tiling needs at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma (Graham–Pollak).**  \nLet the edges of \\(K_n\\) be partitioned into \\(m\\) complete bipartite subgraphs \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_m\\).  \nThen \\(m\\ge n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\) choose a vector \\(x_k\\in\\mathbb R^{\\,n}\\) defined by  \n\n\\[\nx_k(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\n+1 &\\text{if vertex }i\\text{ lies in the first part of }B_k,\\\\[2pt]\n-1 &\\text{if vertex }i\\text{ lies in the second part of }B_k,\\\\[2pt]\n0  &\\text{if }i\\text{ is isolated in }B_k .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nConsider the matrix  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}x_kx_k^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Off‑diagonal entries.*  \nFix distinct \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nExactly one subgraph, say \\(B_{k_0}\\), contains the edge \\(\\{i,j\\}\\).  \nIn that subgraph the two vertices belong to opposite parts, whence \\(x_{k_0}(i)x_{k_0}(j)=-1\\).  \nIn every other subgraph at least one of \\(i,j\\) is isolated, giving product \\(0\\).  \nThus \\(M_{ij}=-1\\) for all \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n*Diagonal entries.*  \nFor each \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nM_{ii}=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}x_k(i)^2\n      =d_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d_i\\) is the number of subgraphs that contain vertex \\(i\\) (i.e. for which \\(x_k(i)\\neq0\\)).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nM = D - J,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\dots ,d_n)\\) and \\(J\\) the all‑ones matrix.\n\nSince each vertex of \\(K_n\\) is incident to \\(n-1\\) edges, it must belong to at least one subgraph; therefore every \\(d_i\\ge1\\) and \\(D\\) is a diagonal matrix of full rank \\(n\\).\n\nThe matrix \\(J\\) has rank \\(1\\).  From (2),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=\\operatorname{rank}(D-J)\n\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(D)-\\operatorname{rank}(J)=n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, each summand \\(x_kx_k^{\\mathsf T}\\) in (1) has rank \\(1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}(x_kx_k^{\\mathsf T})=m .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) yields \\(m\\ge n-1\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nApplying the lemma to our situation, the number \\(m\\) of undirected complete bipartite subgraphs needed to partition the edges of \\(K_n\\) is at least \\(n-1\\).  \nSince each such subgraph corresponds to a pair of opposite tiles, the number of tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;=\\;2m\\;\\ge\\;2(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling uses **at least** \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) create two tiles:\n\n* **Horizontal tile**  \n  \\[\n  H_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\]\n  a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\n* **Vertical tile**  \n  \\[\n  V_i:=\\{(j,i)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\]\n  a rectangle of size \\((n-i)\\times 1\\).\n\nAll tiles are axis‑parallel, their sides lie on grid lines, and they are pairwise disjoint:\n\n* Two horizontal tiles \\(H_i\\) and \\(H_{i'}\\) lie in distinct rows, so they do not intersect.\n* Two vertical tiles \\(V_i\\) and \\(V_{i'}\\) lie in distinct columns, so they do not intersect.\n* A horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) occupies row \\(i\\) and columns \\(>i\\); a vertical tile \\(V_{i'}\\) occupies column \\(i'\\) and rows \\(>i'\\).  \n  If \\(i=i'\\) the two tiles are the prescribed pair \\((H_i,V_i)\\) and intersect only at the cell \\((i,i)\\), which is uncovered.  \n  If \\(i\\neq i'\\) then either the row of \\(H_i\\) is not among the rows of \\(V_{i'}\\) or the column of \\(V_{i'}\\) is not among the columns of \\(H_i\\); hence the tiles are disjoint.\n\nEvery off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to the horizontal tile \\(H_i\\); every off‑diagonal cell with \\(i>j\\) belongs to the vertical tile \\(V_j\\).  \nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles cover **all** cells except the diagonal ones, and each row (resp. column) contains exactly one uncovered cell \\((i,i)\\).\n\nConsequently a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction of §5 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
